A||||6||engine18||Wheel Wisdom?||||5||
Z||000000||engine18||12-11-2000||10:23 AM||||Will these wheels/tires fit my 84 2m4???<P>Wheels?<BR>17" X 6 1/2, 5 lugs, 4 1/2" Center<P>Tires?<BR>Goodyear Eagles LS (225/55/17)<P>Thanks For Any Wisdom......||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||12-11-2000||10:25 AM||||What is the lugnut pattern? The fiero is 5 X 100mm. (5 lugs, 100mm's apart) If the wheels you are looking at have 4 1/2" pattern it won't fit a Fiero.<P>225 tires require 6" minumum and 8" maximum.<BR>These are tire manufacturer's recommended specs.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||12-11-2000||11:23 AM||||can't find my calulator but on a guess i'd think 225/55-17 will be too large in total dia. try this.<P>255x .03937= section width in inches<BR>secton width x.55 = side wall height<BR>side wall heightx2= top and bottom side wall heights combined. add 17 to get total tire height. this will just give you a general idea since the measurements will vary by tire manufacturer.<P>again guessing with no calc. i'd guess at a 40-45 series if you wanted to stay with a 255-17" tire. hopefully if i explained the formula well enough you can plug in numbers to see what should work.   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||horseman||12-11-2000||10:08 PM||||Hey Phil,<BR>explain your formula to me. The one that I am familiar with goes like this: 225(tread width in mm) x .55(aspect ratio)<BR>divide by 25.4(constant) multiply times 2 and add 17(rim size)=26.744 inches. I am always ready to learn.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||12-11-2000||10:30 PM||||Hey, check out this website: <A HREF="http://www.thefieroclub.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thefieroclub.com</A>   and go to the Tire Calculator.  This program is awesome for checking tire sizes.  As far as wheels go, not only is the bolt spacing important, but also the back spacing.  Back spacing can immensely affect the usefulness/looks of the wheels.  If they stick out too much they look kinda stupid and may require fender extensions by law.  If they stick in too far they will rub on anything that gets in the way, which is usually inner fenders first.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||12-11-2000||10:41 PM||||horseman, it looks the same except that you did the math in a slightly different order. looks like you're doing part of it in mm then convertng to inches where i change to inches first. the result is the same. at least i think it will be. still can't find my calc. at best if you change to inches first you get an idea of the width of the tire in inches. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||12-11-2000||11:03 PM||||yep. found my calc. 26.744075. ||||reg||1
